pretation of the findings\nHow to apply\nExplore the Environment, Society, and Sustainability Institute\nWork with faculty with wide ranging expertise\nOur environmental science faculty conduct research in a number of areas including applied ecology, atmospheric chemistry, biogeochemistry, bioremediation and environmental microbiology, conservation biology, contaminant fate and transport, forest ecology, environmental chemistry, GIS applications, global climate change, surface and groundwater hydrology, limnology, mathematical biology and statistics, meteorology and climatology, and toxicology and developmental neurobiology. Dissertation research may be interdisciplinary or may focus on a sub-discipline of environmental science. Specific research projects are highlighted in individual faculty profiles.\nExplore our research areas\nFeatured faculty focused on environmental science\nMeet all environmental science faculty\nAdmissions information\nApplicants to this program should possess a baccalaureate degree in science, mathematics, engineering, or a closely related field. A Master\u2019s degree is not required for admission. A statement of research interests is a key component of the admissions application and is used to match your research interests with those of one or more faculty members. Your statement should refer to a specific area (or areas) of research interest and identify potential research mentors by name. It is highly recommended to initiate contact with potential advisors prior to application, as students are not admitted unless there is a match with a faculty.\nHow to Apply\nDegree requirements\nThe Ph.D. in Environmental Science requires the completion of 90 credit hours in advanced study and research beyond the bachelor's degree. A student must complete 30 credit hours of advanced course work and 30 credit hours of research, typically taken as SPEA-E 625. The student, with approval of his/her advisory committee, should complete a combination of additional course work and research credit to meet the 90-credit hour requirement.\nCourse Requirements\nThe exact nature and amount of course work in each of three areas, principal field of study, breadth in environmental science, and research methods, will be determined by the Advisory Committee after review and approval of the student's plan of study.\nAdequate knowledge for qualifying examinations\nAppropriate preparation for a research project\nA mixture of courses that meet the individual professional goals of the student\nFields of Study\nEach student should define a principal field of study which may be interdisciplinary. The student should prepare a proposal outlining a program of course work that the student believes lies within that principal field.\nEach student should also prepare a program of course work that fulfills the requirement of breadth in environmental science and policy. The breadth requirement may be fulfilled by using a wide spectrum of environmentally related courses, including areas such as economics, law, and management, in addition to other science courses.\nEach student is also required to prepare a statement of courses or activities for meeting the research methods requirement. Normally these include subjects such as computer science, geographic information systems, remote sensing, statistics, and mathematical modeling, although other technical skill areas such as electronics and analytical chemical techniques may be appropriate for some students.\nHow to Apply\nFeatured environmental science alumni\nPaige Price, Ph.D.'24\nAir Quality Consultant, Ramboll (Salt Lake City, UT)\nMeet Paige Price\nSander Denham, Ph.D.'23\nPostdoctoral Ecologist, USDA-ARS\nMeet Sander Denham\nMolly Cain, Ph.D.'22\nAssociate Director for Research, Alaska Sea Grant, University of Alaska Fairbanks\nMeet Molly Cain\nJase Hixson, Ph.D.'22\nSenior Consultant, Antea Group USA\nMeet Jase Hixson\nLienne Sethna, Ph.D.'22\nAssistant Scientist, St. Croix Watershed Research Station - Science Museum of Minnesota\nMeet Lienne Sethna\nWhy O'Neill?\nDegrees\nPh.D. in Public Affairs\nPh.D. in Environmental Science\nHow to Apply\nStudent Funding\nOn the Job Market\nRecent Placements\nCurrent Students\nDissertations\nContact\nPaul H. O\u2019Neill School of Public and Environmental Affairs social media channels\nGive to the O\u2019Neill School\nTwitter\nFacebook\nInstagram\nYouTube\nLinkedIn\nAdditional links and resources\nREPORT A BIAS INCIDENT | O\u2019NEILL SCHOOL DOCUMENTS | FACULTY RESEARCH RESOURCES | INTRANET\nAccessibility |\nCollege Scorecard |\nOpen to All |\nPrivacy Notice\n|\nCopyright \u00a9 2026\nThe Trustees of\nIndiana University", "links": [["Skip to Content", "https://oneill.indiana.edu/doctoral/degrees/environmental-science.html#content"], ["Skip to Main Navigation", "https://oneill.indiana.edu/doctoral/degrees/environmental-science.html#nav-main"], ["Skip to Search", "https://oneill.indiana.edu/doctoral/degrees/environmental-science.html#search"], ["Indiana University Bloomington Indiana University Bloomington IU Bloomington", "https://bloomington.iu.edu"], ["", "https://oneill.indiana.edu/search"], ["Undergraduate", "https://oneill.indiana.edu/undergraduate/index.html"], ["Why O'Neill", "https://oneill.indiana.edu/undergraduate/why-oneill/index.html"], ["High-impact Practices", "https://oneill.indiana.edu/undergraduate/why-oneill/hip.html"], ["Accelerated Degrees", "https://oneill.indiana.edu/undergraduate/academic-enrichment/accelerated-masters/index.html"], ["Degrees & Majors", "https://oneill.indiana.edu/undergraduate/degrees-majors/index.html"], ["Bachelor of Science in Public Affairs (BSPA)", "https://oneill.indiana.edu/undergraduate/degrees-majors/bspa.html"], ["Bachelor of Science in Environmental Science (BSES)", "https://oneill.indiana.edu/undergraduate/degrees-majors/environmental-science.html"], ["Bachelor of Arts in Environmental and Sustainability Studies (BAESS)", "https://oneill.indiana.edu/undergraduate/